In this lesson we'll look at tangents and the gradient function. So here's the key idea: if you pick any two points on a curve and connect them with a straight line, that's called a chord. Now imagine slowly sliding those two points closer together until they merge into one. The chord shrinks into a tangent, a line that just kisses the curve at that single point. The slope of that tangent is what we call the instantaneous rate of change, basically how fast the function is changing right at that moment. Now, the sign of that gradient tells you what the graph is doing. If the gradient is positive, the graph is rising. If it's negative, the graph is falling. And if the gradient is zero, you've hit a stationary point, which just means the tangent is flat, so you're at a peak, a trough, or a flattening inflection. Here's the really cool part. If you record the tangent gradient at every single point along the curve, you get a brand new function, called the gradient function. Where the original graph climbs most steeply, the gradient function is at its biggest. And wherever the original has a turning point, the gradient function hits zero. For real-world data, like a cooling coffee or water filling a tank, you read these ideas qualitatively, looking for steep bits, flat bits, and peaks. Now let's see it in action.